      "report_date": "2026-11-11",
      "title": "Beyond the Signature: How Attackers Hide Malware in PE Certificate Tables",
      "summary": "Morphisec researchers describe a finance-themed campaign, distributed via WhatsApp and email archives with invoice and account-adjustment lures, that delivers a modular encrypted backdoor. Two alternative loaders converge on Donut-based execution: one side-loads a malicious dbghelp.dll beside a Tencent-signed executable and downloads shellcode for Early Bird APC injection into mspaint.exe. The newer variant hides an RC4-encrypted payload in the PE certificate-table region of a modified Kingsoft/WPS DLL and runs it via in-process APC. The implant communicates over AES-256-GCM-protected TCP, profiles hosts, monitors user activity, and loads C2-delivered PE modules for persistence and remote desktop. Defenders should correlate suspicious side-loaded DLLs, unexpected PE security-directory data, and APC-based execution rather than trusting signed hosts alone.",
