      "title": "The global socket: how threat actors are weaponizing GSocket for stealthy C2",
      "summary": "Threat actors are deploying GSocket, an open source remote access tool, on compromised Linux servers to keep access after the original web application vulnerabilities are patched. GSocket relays traffic through a shared public network, so there is no threat actor-owned IP address or domain to block. Microsoft Defender telemetry shows detections rising 356.2% from March through August 2026, concentrated on PHP web applications and cPanel hosting. Education and research accounted for the largest share of affected organizations at 30.7%. Defenders should remediate the host by removing the GSocket client, persistence, and staged files, and constrain outbound traffic rather than relying on application patching alone.",
