    "parsed": {
      "report_date": "2026-11-11",
      "title": "Citrus Ransomware: VLESS for traffic, Sn00py for encryption, and LLMs lending a hand",
      "summary": "Citrus Ransomware is a previously undocumented Russian-speaking group that targets Russian companies only, and it gains access through compromised contractor accounts used over corporate VPNs or internet-facing RDP. The group uses LLM-generated commands and scripts, identifiable by typical LLM comments, and tunnels traffic through Xray VLESS with REALITY to mimic HTTPS. It escalates privileges by exploiting the Certighost authentication bypass (CVE-2026-54121) and disables Windows Defender and other security tools via malicious Group Policy Objects. Its Sn00py-family ransomware, distributed as ransomware-as-a-service, encrypts user files with RSA-2048 and ChaCha20 and skips domain controllers so the policy keeps spreading. Defenders should tightly control contractor access, since compromised contractor accounts are how attackers get in.",
